-- chem/collapse.cub — the collapse stack on chem/linalg.cub (docs/chemical-identity.md, "The remaining stack is one
-- move"). Two parts. (I) Over any type A with a zero, a sum and a product, each law taken as a hypothesis where it is
-- used (a path): the dot product and matrix form (`cl-dot`, `cl-mform`, chem/linalg.cub's at A = BQ in shape); the
-- zero facts M(0, w) = 0 (by computation) and M(u, 0) = 0 (`cl-mform-nil-r`), the zero vector being nil; scaling and
-- additivity of the dot product, hence of a matrix form, in its first argument (`cl-dot-scale`, `cl-dot-add`); and the
-- block-diagonal lemma: for D(u, v) = a·u₀v₀ + C(u′, v′) and a witness ws of C, the witness e₀, (0, w) for w in ws has
-- Gram matrix [[a, 0], [0, Gram C ws]] (`cl-bd-gram`), and its count is the count of C on ws with one more when a is
-- negative, the flag unchanged, for a nonzero (`cl-block-count`, `cl-block-count-ring` from the ring's laws). (II) Over
-- the rationals of chem/binary.cub, by computation: the witness that 1×1 eliminations in order produce (the form's
-- Gram–Schmidt basis of the unit vectors, `cq-gs`), whose Gram matrix on N = q·m − p·1 is diagonal with entries
-- D_k/D_{k−1}, the ratios of the leading minors chem/inertia.cub's elimination returns (`cq-pivots`), and whose count
-- (chem/linalg.cub's `ln-count`) is the count `inertia-below` returns (`cq-agree`): on the three-site path at 3/2 and
-- 5/2 and on a full 4×4 matrix at 1. Not proved: that the laws of (I) hold of the binary rationals (bq-add and bq-mul
-- normalize by a gcd, so they hold only of rationals in lowest terms, and need the gcd's correctness); Sylvester's law,
-- that two diagonalizing witnesses of one form give one count (an instance only, `collapse-two-witnesses`); and (II) for
-- every matrix (it is checked on instances).
import "linalg.hypercubical"

import "inertia.hypercubical"

